“Five nineteen and forty-three seconds.”

Diane checked her watch. “The test ends at five twenty-five.”

Evan looked toward the booth. “Six minutes?”

“Six minutes under controlled conditions. Then we retrieve the camera and leave.”

“You agreed to a locked-booth test.”

“This is the locked-booth test.”

His hands stayed visible at his sides. The discipline cost him effort. He had built the evening around response: questions into corridors, edited phrases, repeated playback, names given to sounds. Without questions or commentary, he lost his strongest method of shaping the session.

Diane turned off the stage work lights.

Emergency strips remained along the aisles, too weak to reveal the seats they bordered. A low green square from the monitor covered the road case and the lower half of Evan’s face. Above the house, the booth opening appeared as a darker rectangle cut into darkness. The camera feed retained more detail than Diane could see from the stage. Low-light processing flattened the console into gray planes and raised faint speckling across the walls, giving every fixed surface a slight, restless motion.

The first minute produced ventilation noise.

Air moved through the auditorium diffusers with a soft, broad rush. The booth microphone added a thinner mechanical tone from the camera’s internal electronics. At intervals, the image brightened and settled as the automatic exposure recalculated the empty room.

Diane named each change for the record.

“Exposure adjustment. Internal fan. House ventilation.”

Evan whispered, “You’re still cueing interpretation.”

“I’m recording conditions.”

“Your labels carry authority.”

“Then challenge them during review.”

The monitor brightened once more. A pale vertical band crossed the left side of the booth frame and dissolved near the console.

Abby shifted her weight. “Light change.”

Diane studied the stage, the house doors, and the rear wall. A narrow spill from the exit sign touched the booth opening. Dust moved through the projector beam from the monitor.

“Auto exposure,” Diane said. “Possible reflection from the house.”

Evan kept his gaze on the screen. “Reflection from what?”

“Camera lens, console surface, exit light, moving dust.”

“Nothing moved.”

“The image changed.”

The next exposure adjustment arrived in two pulses. The first washed the booth wall. The second gathered beside the door and held through three frames.

A pale interruption occupied head height in the image.

A shoulder-width band followed, edgeless and less a body than a refusal of the wall behind it.

Then the camera corrected, and the image showed the empty doorway.

Evan inhaled through his teeth.

Abby kept the main camera steady. “Time.”

“Five twenty-one and nine seconds,” Diane said.

The booth timecode showed five twenty-one and eight.

“The offset still appears within about one second,” she added.

Evan pointed at the monitor. “That crossed in front of the door.”

“It occupied part of the image.”

“You saw a person.”

“I saw a light form.”

“Inside a locked room.”

“The room contains reflective surfaces and an active camera choosing its own exposure.”

The pale image change returned near the console.

This time it moved across the frame from right to left. Its lower section broke into square compression blocks. The upper portion remained smooth for half a second, then stretched as the camera dropped frames.

A sound entered through the monitor speaker.

Evan’s voice.

“Can you hear me?”

He stood beside Diane with his mouth closed.

The phrase came through the booth microphone in a compressed whisper, thin at the beginning and clearer on hear me. Abby’s main camera captured Evan turning toward the monitor.

Diane raised one hand. “Positions stay fixed.”

“That’s me,” Evan said.

“Stay where you are.”

“I said that during setup.”

“When?”

“Before we locked the booth. I checked the microphone from the console.”

Abby answered from behind the camera. “Around five twelve.”

Diane watched the timecode. “The live feed may carry buffer delay. The camera may also contain a playback or monitoring function.”

“Seven minutes is no buffer,” Evan said.

“Wireless interference, cached audio, internal review mode, cross-talk from the laptop receiver. We inspect settings after retrieval.”

The monitor released a short burst of static.

Under it came a second speech-like fragment.

“Come here.”

Diane’s fingers closed around the edge of the road case.

To Diane, the fragment carried Wayne’s rise on here. The sound broke before the next breath. Static filled the gap.

Then Diane heard: “I have a joke.”

Evan stared at her.

Their order made the two phrases sound like an exchange.

Can you hear me?

Come here. I have a joke.

Three seconds separated them. The first phrase came from Evan’s earlier microphone check; the second matched the recorder file already heard and copied. A camera, receiver, or laptop could have passed both through the monitor. Prior playback could have entered the booth microphone during setup. Everyone present already knew the wording before the test began.

The pale image change crossed the feed again.

Its passage coincided with a bright pulse from the exit sign during the scheduled change to evening power. The monitor image fractured, recovered, and showed the empty door.

Diane reached for the laptop.

Evan stepped between her and the keyboard. “Leave it running.”

“I’m checking the receiver source.”

“You’ll contaminate the test.”

“The test already contains your voice and a copied phrase.”

“That is the result.”

“That is an audio path requiring inspection.”

Abby said, “Evan, move.”

He remained in place.

Diane pointed toward the main camera. “Your obstruction is documented. Move now.”

He shifted aside.

The laptop showed the live receiver application, one connected camera, and an audio meter still moving. A thumbnail cache ran along the bottom edge. Diane touched no controls. She read the screen aloud.

“Live mode. One camera connected. Audio active. Cache thumbnails present.”

“Those are live thumbnails,” Evan said.

“They are stored images from the stream.”

“Images, not audio.”

“We inspect the cache later.”

The booth speaker produced a low scrape.

On the feed, the door handle moved downward.

Abby stepped closer to the road case while keeping the camera on her shoulder. “The handle moved.”

Diane watched it rise to level. “Latch tension. Door closer pressure. Temperature change in the lock case.”

The handle moved again, farther this time.

A bright line appeared between door and jamb. The door opened a fraction of an inch and stopped against the deadbolt.

Evan whispered, “It is locked.”

“The latch has play against the bolt.”

“From inside?”

“From pressure across the door.”

The line closed.

A dull impact sounded from the booth floor.

Diane checked the time. “Five twenty-three and eighteen.”

Evan’s hands rose from his sides. “Ask who is there.”

“The conditions remain unchanged.”

“We already have a response.”

“We have two familiar recordings in sequence.”

“Then one question gives us a comparison.”

“Silence continues until retrieval.”

The monitor speaker crackled.

A fragment emerged beneath the noise.

“—up—”

Evan looked toward the booth opening.

A second fragment followed.

“—here—”

The words could have come from his edited clip, air movement, electrical noise, or the camera’s internal processing. After several playbacks, Diane heard the phrase in the fragments.

Up here.

Diane checked the stage floor around the road case. All cables remained visible. Abby stood near the apron. Evan stood within reach of the laptop. Both phones remained on the table.

“Test ends now,” Diane said.

“Two minutes remain.”

“The audio path has compromised the conditions.”

“That means we inspect it while recording.”

“We retrieve the camera under continuous coverage.”

She turned on the stage work lights. The work lights revealed the auditorium in pale rows. Abby adjusted exposure and kept the booth opening, Diane, Evan, and the monitor in frame.

Diane lifted the road case handle. “Abby follows. Evan stays between us. Laptop remains here.”

“You need the monitor.”

“The booth remains visible from the house.”

They moved up the center aisle together. Their shadows crossed the seat backs. Diane watched the booth opening above them and counted each person’s position through Abby’s lens reflected in the monitor.

At the rear aisle, the booth stairs rose behind a fire door. Diane opened it and switched on the landing light. The narrow stairwell contained painted block, a metal handrail, and the smell of heated dust from old dimmer equipment.

The booth door stood at the top.

The seal strip she had placed across the jamb remained intact. Her initials crossed the tape. Evan’s signature and Abby’s camera time were still visible beside them.

Diane held the assigned booth key beneath Abby’s lens.

“Key number B-seventeen. Seal intact. Time five twenty-four and six seconds.”

She inserted the key.

It entered fully.

The cylinder stayed fixed.

Diane withdrew it, checked the cuts, and inserted it again with slight upward pressure. The key moved a fraction and stopped.

Evan said, “You opened it ten minutes ago.”

“I locked it ten minutes ago.”

“Same key?”

“Same key.”

She tried the handle. The deadbolt held. The latch carried the same small play seen on the feed.

“Lock may have shifted under door pressure,” Diane said. “Cylinder pins may be binding.”

“Or the key changed.”

“The key is documented on Abby’s recording.”

“Then the lock changed.”

“Metal changes with heat, wear, pressure, and alignment.”

A soft scrape sounded beyond the door.

All three faced it.

The main camera’s red light reflected in the lock plate. Abby’s breathing remained controlled. Evan stood one step below Diane, his hands visible.

The scrape crossed from the booth door toward the glassless opening.

Diane pulled the key free.

“We use the maintenance access.”

Evan looked toward the ceiling. “There is another route?”

“A service crawlspace reaches the booth wall.”

“You said the booth was isolated.”

“I said the door was locked and the room inspected. The crawlspace panel is bolted from the maintenance side.”

“So exclusion was incomplete.”

“All buildings contain service routes.”

The monitor speaker, still audible from the stage below, produced another burst of static.

A pale light filled the glassless opening above the auditorium.

From the stair landing, Diane could see the booth interior through that opening at an angle. The console edge appeared first. Then the back of a gray plastic chair rose into view.

It stood directly behind the empty window frame, facing the stage.

A strip of orange repair tape circled its right rear leg.

# **Scene 9 — The Key That Is Not Hers**

Evan moved toward the booth door.

Diane blocked the top stair with her body.

“Stay on the landing.”

“The chair came from a locked cage.” His voice carried excitement stripped of caution. “We need the camera before the file changes.”

“The file remains on the card.”

“You have a key that stopped working.”

“I have a binding cylinder.”

He looked at the narrow glassless opening, then at the tripod visible behind it. “I can reach the window from the balcony rail.”

“You can fall twenty feet into the house.”

Abby kept the main camera on them. “Evan, stay here.”

He glanced down the stairs. The tripod’s aluminum legs offered leverage against the door if he chose force. Diane recognized the calculation before his hands moved.

She took the tripod from him and passed it to Abby.

“The booth remains sealed at this door,” Diane said. “I am opening the maintenance side. Abby records the seal, the window, and Evan. Evan keeps both hands visible.”

“What records you?” he asked.

“No one.”

“That breaks continuity.”

“It preserves the room.”

His hand went to the empty pocket, then stopped. His phone still sat onstage. Frustration crossed his face.

Diane turned toward the stairs. “Stay together.”

The scrape inside the booth resumed.

One chair leg crossed the floor. A pause. Another scrape followed at a different angle.

